cartridge "NUMBER PLEASE?/COMPUTE'S GAZETTE/1987"

' NUMBER PLEASE?
' A ColecoVision game based on "Number Please?", COMPUTE!'s Gazette,
' October 1987 (Commodore 64 and 128, BASIC; the listing names no author).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a 3 x 3 cross-number puzzle. Nine digits wait in the tray;
' put each one in a square so that every row and every column works out,
' worked left to right and top to bottom (no precedence), to the total
' shown at its end. Check when the board is full.
'
' From the original (orig/number please.bas, one listing for the C64 and
' C128): the nine digits are random 1-9 (repeats allowed); each row and
' column gets two signs drawn at random from + - x / (the original's draw
' order: rows, then columns; a sign is drawn again until the step stays a
' whole number of at least 1; all six signs are drawn again while a total
' is above 99); the tray holds the nine digits in random places; LEVEL
' 0-3 is the number of squares given (a given square is locked and its
' digit leaves the tray); picking a digit for a square that holds one sends
' that one back to the tray; a full board is checked; CORRECT / INCORRECT;
' an incorrect board can be tried again (the same puzzle); giving up shows
' the answer.
'
' Liberties: the joystick picks up and puts down keys (stick moves the
' cursor, a button picks up or puts down, the CHECK plate checks) instead
' of typing digits, and the board is not cleared after a wrong check. Any
' arrangement that makes all six lines right is accepted (the original only
' accepted its own arrangement; arithmetic with exact division, left to
' right). A game is five calls (puzzles) with a score: each call has three
' checks; a right board scores 25 x (4 - level) x (4 - check number);
' after a wrong check each line shows a tick or a cross; a third wrong
' check shows the answer and moves on (so guessing or sweeping
' arrangements cannot win). The original never ended and kept no score.
' Level is called HINTS on the title. The art (switchboard keys, enamel
' plates, lamp totals), sounds and the title tune are new.
'
' Controls: either controller; stick and side buttons only. Title: up/down
' choose a line, left/right change the hints, a button starts (HOW TO PLAY
' shows two pages of rules, CREDITS the credits). The cursor starts on HINTS.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off,
' display off. Music first in the sound table, the title tune once.

